\begin{remark}[Elliptic-bundle and Hopf degeneration inputs]
\hypertarget{tag-PT10}{}\label{I:elliptic-bundle-primary-comparisons}
\StatementRecord{I:elliptic-bundle-primary-comparisons}{proved}{PT10}{\citep[Definition~1.1 and Remark~1.4, pp.~85--86; Proposition~1.14 and Example~1.15, pp.~93--94]{Fujimoto1990AB}; \citet[Theorem~1.4, preprint p.~7, and Example~2.1, p.~13]{Nishiguchi1988IIRoot}; \citet[Propositions~3.1--3.4, preprint pp.~6--9]{SchroeerSiebert2002Root}}
The Cartier-divisor construction used in Proposition~\ref{push:src:B.4.7} is the construction of \citet[Definition~1.1, pp.~85--86]{Fujimoto1990AB}. His Remark~1.4 attributes non-K\"ahler torus degenerations to Ueno. His Proposition~1.14, p.~93, extends principal elliptic bundles across subsets of codimension at least three; Example~1.15, pp.~93--94, gives a principal-bundle extension failure in codimension two and leaves general relative compactification open in that example. Hopf components occur in the semistable degenerations of \citet[Theorem~1.4, preprint p.~7, and Example~2.1, p.~13]{Nishiguchi1988IIRoot}. The diagonal Hopf quotients, elliptic-conductor identifications and normal lines in \citet[Propositions~3.1--3.4, preprint pp.~6--9]{SchroeerSiebert2002Root} provide related explicit models. Proposition~\ref{push:src:B.4.7} proves its own proper family using the stated Cartier-divisor cocycle.
\end{remark}

